.TH FREE 3crunch "" "Crunch" "Crunch Library Manual"
.SH NAME
free \- release a block of memory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <stdlib.h>
.PP
.BI "void free(void* " address ");"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR free ()
releases a block returned by Crunch
.BR malloc (3crunch)
or
.BR realloc (3crunch).
A null pointer has no effect.
The pointer must be the current allocation pointer, not an interior pointer
or a pointer already freed.
After the call, do not access the allocation or pass its pointer to
.BR free ()
or
.BR realloc ().
.SH RETURN VALUE
The function returns no value.
Successful calls, including
.BR free(0) ,
leave
.B errno
unchanged.
If an underlying unmap fails,
.B errno
is set to the Linux error number.
Set
.B errno
to zero before the call if the caller needs to detect such a failure.
.PP
An empty pool arena that cannot be unmapped remains available for reuse.
Regardless of an unmap failure, the caller must treat the supplied pointer
as invalid after
.BR free ().
.SH NOTES
Crunch
.B errno
is process\-wide, not thread\-local, and the allocator is not thread\-safe.
Do not mix Crunch allocation pointers with the system C allocator or
.BR munmap .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR malloc (3crunch),
.BR realloc (3crunch),
.BR allocator_trim (3crunch),
.BR crunch (1)
